WEBVTT

00:00.160 --> 00:02.170
- [Instructor] So you see
here, this is a screenshot

00:02.170 --> 00:04.510
of the Desmos online graphing calculator.

00:04.510 --> 00:06.620
You can use it at desmos.com,

00:06.620 --> 00:08.540
and I encourage you to
use this after this video,

00:08.540 --> 00:10.370
or even while I'm doing this video,

00:10.370 --> 00:14.580
but the goal here is to think
about reflection of functions.

00:14.580 --> 00:16.660
So let's just start with some examples.

00:16.660 --> 00:19.260
Let's saying that I
had a function, f of x,

00:19.260 --> 00:24.200
and it is equal to the square root of x.

00:24.200 --> 00:26.010
So that's what it looks like.

00:26.010 --> 00:28.030
Fairly reasonable.

00:28.030 --> 00:30.790
Now, let's make another function, g of x,

00:30.790 --> 00:32.840
and I'll start off by also making

00:32.840 --> 00:34.740
that the square root of x.

00:34.740 --> 00:37.500
So no surprise there, g of x was graphed

00:37.500 --> 00:39.510
right on top of f of x.

00:39.510 --> 00:42.170
But what would happen if instead of it

00:42.170 --> 00:43.390
just being the square root of x,

00:43.390 --> 00:45.610
what would happen if we
put a negative out front

00:45.610 --> 00:46.640
right over there?

00:46.640 --> 00:49.723
What do you think is going
to happen when I do that?

00:50.910 --> 00:52.440
Well, let's just try it out.

00:52.440 --> 00:53.740
When I put the negative,

00:53.740 --> 00:57.570
it looks like it flipped
it over the x-axis.

00:57.570 --> 01:02.040
It looks like it reflected
it over the x-axis.

01:02.040 --> 01:04.400
Now instead of doing that way,

01:04.400 --> 01:06.980
what if we had another function, h of x,

01:06.980 --> 01:10.810
and I'll start off by making
it identical to f of x.

01:10.810 --> 01:12.490
So once again, it's right over there.

01:12.490 --> 01:14.620
It traces out f of x.

01:14.620 --> 01:16.290
Instead of putting the negative out

01:16.290 --> 01:18.080
in front of the radical sign,

01:18.080 --> 01:19.750
what if we put it under the radical sign?

01:19.750 --> 01:23.050
What if we replaced x with a negative x?

01:23.050 --> 01:25.610
What do you think is
going to happen there?

01:25.610 --> 01:27.630
Well, let's try it out.

01:27.630 --> 01:28.990
If we replace it,

01:28.990 --> 01:32.780
that shifted it over the y-axis.

01:32.780 --> 01:33.980
And then, pause this video,

01:33.980 --> 01:36.403
and think about how you
shifted over both axes.

01:37.310 --> 01:38.730
Well, we could do a,

01:38.730 --> 01:40.130
well, I'm running out of letters,

01:40.130 --> 01:43.870
maybe I will do a, I don't
know, k of x is equal to,

01:43.870 --> 01:46.600
so I'm gonna put the negative
outside the radical sign,

01:46.600 --> 01:48.810
and then, I'm gonna take the square root,

01:48.810 --> 01:50.980
and I'm gonna put a negative
inside the radical sign.

01:50.980 --> 01:53.200
And notice, it flipped it over both.

01:53.200 --> 01:56.750
It flipped it over both
the x-axis and the y-axis

01:56.750 --> 01:58.270
to go over here.

01:58.270 --> 02:00.020
Now, why does this happen?

02:00.020 --> 02:02.000
Well, let's just start with the g of x.

02:02.000 --> 02:04.200
So when you get put the
negative out in front,

02:04.200 --> 02:06.640
when you negate everything
that's in the expression

02:06.640 --> 02:08.150
that defines a function,

02:08.150 --> 02:11.630
whatever value you would've
gotten of the function before,

02:11.630 --> 02:14.600
you're now going to
get the opposite of it.

02:14.600 --> 02:17.660
So when x is zero, we get zero.

02:17.660 --> 02:19.670
When x is one, instead of one now,

02:19.670 --> 02:20.610
you're taking the negative of it

02:20.610 --> 02:22.300
so you're gonna get negative one.

02:22.300 --> 02:25.820
When x is four, instead
of getting positive two,

02:25.820 --> 02:27.970
you're now going to get negative two.

02:27.970 --> 02:30.600
When x is equal to nine, instead
of getting positive three,

02:30.600 --> 02:32.310
you now get negative three.

02:32.310 --> 02:33.350
So hopefully, that makes sense

02:33.350 --> 02:35.020
why putting a negative out front

02:35.020 --> 02:38.790
of an entire expression
is going to flip it over,

02:38.790 --> 02:42.040
flip its graph over the x-axis.

02:42.040 --> 02:45.060
Now what about replacing
an x with a negative x?

02:45.060 --> 02:46.610
Well, one way to think about it,

02:46.610 --> 02:50.300
now is, whenever you inputted one before,

02:50.300 --> 02:52.510
that would now be a negative one

02:52.510 --> 02:54.670
that you're trying to
evaluate the principle root of

02:54.670 --> 02:56.290
and we know that the
principle root function

02:56.290 --> 02:58.860
is not defined for negative one.

02:58.860 --> 03:01.630
But when x is equal to negative one,

03:01.630 --> 03:03.570
our original function wasn't defined there

03:03.570 --> 03:05.560
when x is equal to negative one,

03:05.560 --> 03:07.660
but if you take the negative of that,

03:07.660 --> 03:09.680
well now you're taking
this principle root of one.

03:09.680 --> 03:12.240
And so, that's why this is now defined.

03:12.240 --> 03:15.550
So, whatever value the
function would've taken on

03:15.550 --> 03:18.352
at a given value of x,
it now takes that value

03:18.352 --> 03:21.370
on the corresponding opposite value of x,

03:21.370 --> 03:23.640
and on the negative value of that x.

03:23.640 --> 03:27.410
And so that's why it
flips it over the y-axis.

03:27.410 --> 03:29.490
And this is true with
many types of functions.

03:29.490 --> 03:33.170
We don't have to do this just
with a square root function.

03:33.170 --> 03:35.300
Let's try another function.

03:35.300 --> 03:40.300
Let's say, we tried this
for e to the x power.

03:42.770 --> 03:43.640
So there you go.

03:43.640 --> 03:46.070
We have a very classic exponential there.

03:46.070 --> 03:49.610
Now let's say that g of x is
equal to negative e to the x.

03:49.610 --> 03:52.140
And if what we expect to happen happens,

03:52.140 --> 03:55.450
this will flip it over the x-axis.

03:55.450 --> 03:59.140
So negative e to the x power

03:59.140 --> 04:01.370
and indeed that is what happens.

04:01.370 --> 04:05.580
And then, how would we
flip it over the y-axis?

04:05.580 --> 04:08.270
Well, let's do an h of x.

04:08.270 --> 04:11.660
That's going to be equal to e to the,

04:11.660 --> 04:13.130
instead of putting an x there,

04:13.130 --> 04:15.660
we will put a negative x.

04:15.660 --> 04:16.720
Negative x.

04:16.720 --> 04:17.580
And there you have it.

04:17.580 --> 04:20.450
Notice, it flipped it over the y-axis.

04:20.450 --> 04:21.980
Now, both examples that I just did,

04:21.980 --> 04:23.360
these are very simple expressions.

04:23.360 --> 04:26.730
Let's imagine something that's
a little bit more complex.

04:26.730 --> 04:28.110
Let's say that f of x,

04:28.110 --> 04:30.840
let's give it a nice,
higher-degree polynomial,

04:30.840 --> 04:33.190
so let's say it's x to the third

04:33.190 --> 04:36.970
minus two x squared.

04:36.970 --> 04:38.200
That's a nice one

04:38.200 --> 04:40.340
and actually let's just
add another term here.

04:40.340 --> 04:43.080
So plus two x.

04:43.080 --> 04:43.913
And I wanna make it,

04:43.913 --> 04:45.340
make it minus two x.

04:45.340 --> 04:47.170
I wanna see it accentuates
some of those curves.

04:47.170 --> 04:49.750
All right, so that's a
pretty interesting graph.

04:49.750 --> 04:54.750
Now, how would I flip it over the x-axis?

04:55.030 --> 04:56.770
Well the way that I would do that

04:56.770 --> 04:59.440
is I could define a g of x.

04:59.440 --> 05:00.470
I could do it two ways.

05:00.470 --> 05:05.100
I could say g of x is equal
to the negative of f of x

05:05.100 --> 05:06.230
and we get that.

05:06.230 --> 05:08.410
So that's essentially just
taking this entire expression

05:08.410 --> 05:10.140
and multiplying it by negative one.

05:10.140 --> 05:11.760
And notice, it's multiplying,

05:11.760 --> 05:14.790
it's flipping it over the x-axis.

05:14.790 --> 05:16.820
Another way we could've
done it is instead of that,

05:16.820 --> 05:21.080
we could've said the
negative of x to the third

05:21.080 --> 05:26.020
minus two x squared,

05:26.020 --> 05:29.010
and then minus two x,

05:29.010 --> 05:31.110
and then we close those parentheses,

05:31.110 --> 05:33.330
and we get the same effect.

05:33.330 --> 05:36.110
Now, what if we wanted to
flip it over the y-axis?

05:36.110 --> 05:37.960
Well then instead of putting a negative

05:37.960 --> 05:39.660
on the entire expression,

05:39.660 --> 05:43.590
what we wanna do is replace
our x's with a negative x.

05:43.590 --> 05:45.260
So you could do it like this.

05:45.260 --> 05:49.020
You could say that that's
going to be f of negative x

05:49.020 --> 05:50.910
and that has the effect
of everywhere you saw

05:50.910 --> 05:53.230
an x before you replaced
it with a negative x.

05:53.230 --> 05:55.570
And notice, it did exactly what we expect.

05:55.570 --> 05:59.920
It flipped it over over the y-axis.

05:59.920 --> 06:01.690
Now, the other way we could've don't that

06:01.690 --> 06:02.760
just to make it clear,

06:02.760 --> 06:05.990
that's the same thing as
negative x to the third power

06:06.920 --> 06:11.140
minus two times negative x squared

06:12.400 --> 06:15.990
minus two times negative x.

06:15.990 --> 06:17.810
And of course, we could
simplify that expression,

06:17.810 --> 06:20.690
but notice, it has the exact same idea.

06:20.690 --> 06:22.130
And if we wanted to flip it over

06:22.130 --> 06:25.160
both the x and y-axis,

06:25.160 --> 06:27.190
well we've already flipped
it over the y-axis,

06:27.190 --> 06:28.780
to flip it over the x-axis,

06:28.780 --> 06:30.390
oh whoops, I just deleted it,

06:30.390 --> 06:33.620
to flip it over the,
I'm having issues here,

06:33.620 --> 06:37.360
to flip it over the x-axis as well,

06:37.360 --> 06:39.710
we would, oh and it gave
me a parentheses already,

06:39.710 --> 06:41.810
I would just put a negative out front.

06:41.810 --> 06:44.440
So I put a negative out
front and there you have it.

06:44.440 --> 06:47.740
This flipped it over
both the x and y-axis.

06:47.740 --> 06:49.210
You can do them in either order

06:49.210 --> 06:51.970
and you will get to this green curve.

06:51.970 --> 06:53.690
Now, an easier way of writing that

06:53.690 --> 06:58.007
would've been just the
negative of f of negative x

06:59.340 --> 07:02.860
and you would've gotten
to that same place.

07:02.860 --> 07:05.270
So go to Desmos, play around with it,

07:05.270 --> 07:07.120
really good to build this intuition,

07:07.120 --> 07:09.253
and really understand why it's happening.
